# What must NOT reach the npm tarball — and adding this file changes the default.
#
# WITHOUT a .npmignore, npm falls back to .gitignore. Creating one REPLACES that
# fallback outright, so everything .gitignore was keeping out of the tarball has to be
# restated here or it silently starts shipping. That is the trap this file is most
# likely to spring, so the .gitignore entries are repeated below rather than assumed.
#
# `files` in package.json is the PRIMARY gate and an allowlist, which is the stronger
# of the two: anything it does not name never ships, whatever this file says. This is
# the second gate, and it earns its place twice — `files` names DIRECTORIES (`js/src`),
# so it cannot exclude something added inside one, and this file is what still holds
# if `files` is ever loosened or dropped.
#
# Checked rather than trusted: the release workflow runs `npm pack --dry-run` and
# prints the tarball's contents before anything is published.

# --- the Python half. Same repository, different registry. ------------------- #
python/
pyproject.toml
MANIFEST.in

# --- tests. The suites are run from the repo, never from the tarball. -------- #
js/test/

# --- development and CI, none of which a consumer installs ------------------- #
.github/
.gitignore
.npmignore
CONTRIBUTING.md
Dockerfile
action.yml
assay.json

# --- restated from .gitignore, because this file replaces that fallback ------ #
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
node_modules/
dist/
build/
*.egg-info/
.coverage

# --- editor and OS droppings ------------------------------------------------- #
.DS_Store
*.swp
*.tgz
.vscode/
.idea/
